SSO wave 1 live capture: silent server-completed consent landing
================================================================

Capture 2 of 2, taken 2026-08-29 after the branch was rebased onto main at
5e08d641. Capture 1 (2026-08-23, pre-rebase) is preserved at the bottom of
this file so the two substrates can be compared.

Substrate (2026-08-29)
----------------------
Throwaway local stack, built for this capture and thrown away after it:

  * self-hosted GoTrue v2.189.0, the digest this repository pins in
    deploy/docker/docker-compose.enterprise.yml, with the OAuth 2.1
    authorization server enabled and GOTRUE_OAUTH_SERVER_AUTHORIZATION_PATH
    pointing at /oauth/consent, on its own throwaway Postgres
  * web-console built from THIS BRANCH with Dockerfile.web-console.prod
    (a real `next build` plus `next start`, not the dev server), fronted by
    the same one-origin arrangement Caddyfile.console uses in production:
    /auth/v1/* proxied to GoTrue, everything else to the console
  * Open WebUI (the Hive fork image) as the relying party, registered on
    GoTrue through the same admin OAuth client registration
    scripts/register-owui-oauth-client.py performs, with PKCE S256

Real OAuth 2.1 authorization-code round trips only; no session injected,
nothing stubbed. Sessions minted through the audited one-time-token flow
(tests/e2e/support/live-auth.mjs, driven by
tests/e2e/support/sso-wave1-fixture.mjs). No password was set, read, reset
or rotated for any account, and no shared or deployed environment was
touched: every user, client and key in this capture was created inside the
throwaway stack.

Every URL below has its query string AND fragment stripped before commit;
the same redaction is burned into the screenshot banners. This flow carries
authorization codes and one-time tokens in the query.

BEFORE (tracer baseline, team-lead 2026-08-23)
----------------------------------------------
Repeat sign-in cost 3 HTML loads plus 3 redirects, and the /oauth/consent
stop painted "Checking your authorization request..." plus the Approve/Deny
panel even when an auth.oauth_consents row already existed.

AFTER, PASS 1: first ever consent for a fresh user
--------------------------------------------------
  200 http://localhost:3010/
  302 http://localhost:3010/oauth/oidc/login
  302 http://console.hive.test:8899/auth/v1/oauth/authorize?<redacted>
  200 http://console.hive.test:8899/oauth/consent?<redacted>
  approve buttons: 1
  deny buttons: 1
  password inputs: 0
  after Approve: http://localhost:3010/

The interactive panel still paints exactly once for a genuine first consent,
and Approve completes the round trip into chat. Wave 1 did not break the
approve/deny case. Frame: 01-first-consent-panel.png.

AFTER, PASS 2: repeat entry, only the console ssr session on the device
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
  200 http://localhost:3010/
  302 http://localhost:3010/oauth/oidc/login
  302 http://console.hive.test:8899/auth/v1/oauth/authorize?<redacted>
  307 http://console.hive.test:8899/oauth/consent?<redacted>
  307 http://localhost:3010/oauth/oidc/callback?<redacted>
  200 http://localhost:3010/
  painted consent documents (2xx on /oauth/consent): 0
  approve buttons: 0
  deny buttons: 0
  password inputs: 0
  redirects through the consent landing: 1
  final: http://localhost:3010/

The consent landing answered 307. Zero painted consent documents, zero
Approve or Deny buttons ever rendered, zero password inputs, and exactly one
redirect beyond the authorize round trip: the landing's own hand-off to the
registered callback. Frame: 04-repeat-entry-chat-home.png, taken with an
activated chat account so the destination is the chat home itself.

Note on the destination: DEFAULT_USER_ROLE is "pending" on the deployment as
well as here, so a brand new chat account lands on Open WebUI's activation
notice rather than the chat home until an admin activates it. That is
existing product behaviour and is unrelated to this change; the frame above
uses an activated account so the end state is the one a returning user sees.

